        }
      ],
      "why": "Full pipelining gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax. To clear the 32 MS/s constraint and the 0.44 us p99-batch bound (32 back-to-back results must finish in 32 cycles, so Fmax must be >= ~72 MHz), the datapath must be fast. Keeping n_iter in 12..16 and data_width in 14..20 bounds the per-stage adder/shift depth so Fmax stays comfortably above 72 MHz, while n_iter>=12 with data_width>=14 keeps max_abs_err <= 2^-12. This family is the natural throughput/area anchor of the front."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 20,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, cutting FF count (the dominant area term in a deep pipeline) while still delivering 1 result/cycle, so it keeps the 32 MS/s throughput and the 0.44 us batch bound. Larger m (2..4) lowers area but lengthens the combinational path per stage, which can push Fmax down; the 12..16 n_iter / 14..20 width ranges keep the per-stage logic shallow enough that even m=2..4 should hold Fmax above ~72 MHz. This family maps the area side of the Pareto front that full pipelining cannot reach."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is throughput- and latency-bound: 32 results must arrive within 0.44 us of a 1 us tick, and throughput must be >= 32 MS/s. Only the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can meet this, so I restrict to those two. The binding hidden constraint is Fmax: 32 back-to-back results in 32 cycles needs Fmax >= ~72 MHz, so I keep n_iter (12..16) and data_width (14..20) modest to limit per-stage depth, while n_iter>=12 / width>=14 guarantees max_abs_err <= 2^-12. I sweep angle_guard (-2..4) and frac_guard (0..4) with both rounding modes to map the accuracy-vs-area trade-off that drives the two objectives (min luts_plus_ffs, max accuracy_bits). I split the budget 55/45 between the two families: pipelined as the reliable throughput/area anchor, and pipelined_m (m=2..4) to push the area objective lower via reduced FFs, which is where the Pareto front's area-extreme corner lives."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The exploration has identified a robust feasible Pareto front dominated by the `pipelined_m` family, which satisfies all system and accuracy constraints with significantly lower area (1240-1434 LUTs+FFs) compared to the fully `pipelined` family (1788+ LUTs+FFs). The best design (`pipelined_m` with data_width=17, n_iter=15, m=4) achieves 12.6 accuracy bits (max_abs_err ~2^-12.61), comfortably meeting the <= 2^-12 constraint, while maintaining a throughput of 93.6 MSPS (well above the 32 MSPS requirement) and a system p99 batch latency of 0.161 us (well below the 0.44 us limit). Since this is the final round, the current front is sufficient for selection based on the 'min luts_plus_ffs' rule, which will pick the 1240 LUTs+FFs design."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
